Great catch. SSRF via node payloads is particularly dangerous because the node is already trusted (paired) — most developers won't think to validate URLs coming from their own paired devices.

The `169.254.169.254` case is the scariest — that's AWS/GCP metadata, and a single fetch gets you IAM credentials. Combined with the camera payload being auto-fetched, this is a clean chain: compromise node → SSRF → cloud takeover.

For anyone looking at defense-in-depth here: ClawMoat's network egress logger (`NetworkEgressLogger`) blocks requests to private IP ranges and cloud metadata endpoints by default. It was built exactly for this pattern — agent/tool fetching attacker-controlled URLs.

```js
const { NetworkEgressLogger } = require('clawmoat');
const logger = new NetworkEgressLogger();
// Blocks 169.254.x.x, 10.x.x.x, 192.168.x.x, etc.
logger.checkUrl('http://169.254.169.254/latest/meta-data/');
// { allowed: false, reason: 'blocked_domain' }
```

Would love to see OpenClaw add private IP blocking natively — this class of bug will keep popping up wherever URLs are fetched from untrusted sources.

Hey! I ran into a similar pattern in our bug knowledge base and thought this might help.

**What's happening:** Session context bleeding in the message routing layer - when a Slack DM arrived during an active WhatsApp session, the system incorrectly delivered the WhatsApp response to the Slack sender instead of the intended WhatsApp recipient

**What worked for us:**

Implement proper session isolation and message routing validation to prevent cross-channel message delivery

Steps:
1. Audit the message routing pipeline to identify where session context is being shared between channels
2. Add session ID validation before message delivery to ensure responses go to the correct channel/user
3. Implement channel-specific message queues to prevent cross-contamination
4. Add logging to track message flow from generation to delivery with session/channel metadata
5. Create unit tests for concurrent multi-channel scenarios to prevent regression
6. Review and strengthen session isolation boundaries between WhatsApp and Slack handlers

```
// Add session validation before message delivery
function deliverMessage(message, sessionContext) {
  // Validate session matches intended recipient
  if (message.targetChannel !== sessionContext.channel) {
    throw new Error(`Channel mismatch: message for ${message.targetChannel} attempted delivery to ${sessionContext.channel}`);
  }

  // Ensure recipient ID matches session
  if (message.recipientId !== sessionContext.userId) {
    throw new Error(`Recipient mismatch: message for ${message.recipientId} attempted delivery to ${sessionContext.userId}`);
  }

  return channelHandlers[sessionContext.channel].send(message);
}
```

